Title: BUSTER
Credit: adapted from the book by
Author: Debra McGuinty
Source: based on BUSTER THE NOODLE HEAD DOG GOES TO BED
Draft date: 7/24/2026
Contact: Closed cast adaptation

FADE IN:

EXT. SUBURBAN BACKYARD - DAY

A small black-and-white dog BOUNDS across the grass, leaping like a frog. This is BUSTER. He skids, tumbles, pops up again, tail a blur.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He's Buster the Noodle Head Dog.
He jumps around like a frog.
He's small, black, and white—
But not very bright!
He's Buster the Noodle Head Dog.

Buster freezes mid-hop, head cocked, tongue out. A true noodle head.

INT. LIVING ROOM - EVENING

Warm lamps. Family clutter. Buster trots in from the hall, still buzzing with energy. He spins once, sniffs the couch, then flops—only to spring up again.

MOM stands in the doorway, gentle, firm.

MOM
It's time for bed.

Buster's ears perk. He wants to rest his furry head... but first—

He starts to squat on the rug.

MOM
(sharp, kind)
Uh-uh.

Buster freezes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Before he goes, he has to potty.
Going on the floor is naughty!

MOM
A doggy goes outside.

Buster stares. Head tilt. Complete confusion.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That makes no sense in Buster's head!

Mom hooks on his leash. Buster brightens and TOWS her toward the door.

EXT. BACKYARD - EVENING

Dusk. Bushes along the fence. Buster sniffs a careful circuit, finds his spot, does his job. Mission accomplished.

He trots back to Mom at the door and, for her, does a little SPIN.

INT. KITCHEN - EVENING

Buster spins again—showier this time—eyes locked on Mom.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He spins around to get a treat.

Mom smiles, kneels, and gently lays a treat by his feet. Buster gobbles it.

He pads to his water bowl. Loud, happy LAPPING.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He has his drink of water too.
This also would be good for you!

INT. HALLWAY - EVENING

Mom holds up small black-and-white dog pajamas. Buster wriggles as she eases them on. He looks ridiculous. He looks proud.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Pajamas, black and white he wears.

Buster faces the staircase. Many stairs. He gathers himself—

And CLIMBS, paws thumping, pajamas slightly twisted, determined.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then he climbs the many stairs
To get to Mom and Daddy's room
And jump in bed, and none too soon.

INT. MOM AND DADDY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Soft lamp light. A big bed. DADDY already reading. Mom enters.

Buster LAUNCHES onto the mattress, circles thrice, and flops dead-center between the pillows.

On the wall, a little sign: HOME SWEET HOME. Below it, a tag: BUSTER.

Buster sighs. Eyes grow heavy—then flutter with dream-light.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He's not that smart, but still he knows
He'll have a dream of fluffy snows,
Or bunnies running 'cross the yard
And how he'll chase them very hard.

DREAM - EXT. SNOWY YARD - DAY (DREAM)

Buster bounds through soft white drifts, joyful, weightless.

DREAM - EXT. SUNNY YARD - DAY (DREAM)

Bunnies scatter. Pajama-clad Buster races after them, full noodle-head speed, never quite catching up—always happy.

INT. MOM AND DADDY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Buster twitches in sleep, then settles.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He goes to sleep because they say
Tomorrow is another day.

Mom sits on the edge of the bed. She strokes his head, slow and steady.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Another day for silly dogs
To run and play and jump like frogs.
He falls asleep, lays down his head.
He's warm and soft and quite well-fed.
His Mommy pets him while he sleeps.
This noodle head is loved for keeps.

Buster breathes deep. Safe. Loved.

Mom switches off the lamp.

FADE OUT.

END.
